From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rui Figueira <rfigueira@cloudflare.com>
Date: Tue, 1 Sep 2026 19:36:57 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Support a layout rounding step other than one pixel

The terminal backend needs boxes on cell boundaries rather than pixel
boundaries, and taffy now takes that step from the RoundTree. Store it
per-document so a single process can host both a terminal document and
an ordinary rasterised one; the default is (1, 1), taffy's own
behaviour, so nothing changes for a caller that never sets it.

Under a cell grid, rounding runs after the line break and rounds a box's
left and right edges independently, so a container nothing touched still
commits a width up to one cell from the width its lines were broken at.
The clobber check compared with a half-pixel tolerance, so it declared
every inline root on such a page stale and re-broke it through parley's
break_all_lines, which knows nothing about floats. A floated infobox
therefore stopped shortening any line box: on Wikipedia in the terminal
grid modes the article text ran straight underneath the infobox, and the
words it covered were simply gone from the page.

Tolerating a sub-cell difference costs no correction, because on a grid
where every glyph is one cell wide a width change smaller than a cell
cannot move a break. The half-pixel tolerance stays wherever the grid is
off.

A coarse rounding step (a terminal's character grid) rounds table-cell
boxes but left DetailedGridInfo at fractional coordinates, so collapsed
borders painted off the cell edges. Keep collapsed-border gutters at
pixel width and absorb cumulative grid rounding into adjacent tracks so
table painting follows the cells.

Taffy rounds from document-wide unrounded coordinates, so the table's
content origin includes every ancestor up the containing_block() chain,
the box a location is relative to. A sub-cell inset that layout cannot
round away without making padding negative is subtracted too, so table
rules end on the snapped cell edge.

An ancestor-only move can change the grid phase while the table cache
keeps its tracks, so Taffy's raw track metadata is preserved and a
separate paint copy is derived on each resolve.
